BUILD AND RUN (iLab / Linux with gcc)
    make
    ./memgrind
    make check
    make clean
No programs take arguments except test_errors, described below.
make builds memgrind; make tests builds the two correctness-test programs.
make check builds everything and runs run_tests.sh, including memgrind.
Do not use -DNDEBUG when testing: tests use assert for their checks.

DESIGN
The allocator was rewritten for the Fall 2026 specification after reviewing
https://github.com/chris-gong/mymalloc. Its source is not copied here.
This package was prepared with assistance from OpenAI Codex. Authors should
review and understand the implementation and record any course-required
assistance disclosure before submission.

Storage is a static union with a double member for alignment and a 4096-byte
character array. Exactly one static int tracks initialization. Every chunk
header is stored within the array; there are no external tracking arrays,
linked-list pointers, or allocation counters. Helpers use automatic locals.

Each header is eight bytes, encoded as a uint64_t. Its low bit indicates
allocation; the remaining bits store total chunk length including header.
Headers are read/written with memcpy to avoid object-aliasing violations.
Payloads are rounded up to multiples of eight. The minimum chunk is sixteen
bytes. A first-fit scan chooses a free chunk. Splitting occurs only if the
remainder can contain an eight-byte header and at least eight payload bytes;
otherwise the client receives the entire chunk. Allocated objects never move.

Initialization is automatic in both malloc and free and registers the leak
detector with atexit. free validates exact payload addresses using equality
comparisons, avoiding ordering or subtraction of unrelated pointers. After
freeing, adjacent free chunks coalesce, recovering obsolete header space.
Bad non-NULL pointers print to stderr and terminate with status 2.
Allocation failures print the original requested size and return NULL.
The leak detector counts live payloads (including alignment/unsplit padding),
excluding headers, and does not itself call exit.

Unspecified edge cases: malloc(0) returns NULL with the allocation-failure
diagnostic. free(NULL) is a no-op, matching standard free. Requests too large
for the heap, including SIZE_MAX, are rejected before rounding can overflow.
Use-after-free without an allocator call is outside this library's detection
scope. Once an address is allocated again, an old pointer to that same address
cannot be distinguished from the new pointer by this interface.

MEMLENGTH can be changed in mymalloc.c or overridden at compile time, e.g.
    gcc -std=c11 -DMEMLENGTH=8192 -c mymalloc.c
It must be a multiple of eight and at least sixteen. The supplied tests and
stress workload assume the assignment's default 4096-byte heap.

STRESS WORKLOAD: memgrind
Uses gettimeofday before and after fifty complete runs of the following five
tasks; prints average microseconds per complete workload. Randomness is seeded
once with 214 so choices are reproducible. No task deliberately makes errors.
1. Allocate 8,16,32,64,128,512,1024 bytes, then free in reverse order.
2. Allocate 120 one-byte objects; free in allocation order.
3. Randomly choose allocation or freeing a random live object. Stop after
   120 allocations, then free all remaining objects. With no live objects,
   a deallocation choice does nothing. Live pointers are kept in a packed array.
4. Allocate forty mixed-size objects, release alternating objects, replace
   those with smaller objects, then free using a permutation of the indices.
5. Allocate thirty 64-byte objects; free adjacent pairs in each group of three,
   reuse those pairs for 128-byte objects, and free everything in reverse groups.
Each task frees all objects. Peak live chunk storage stays below 4096 bytes.

TEST PLAN: requirement -> detection method -> executable test
1. Alignment/rounding: allocate sizes 1..24, check each address modulo eight;
   also read/write a double. test_correctness.
2. Non-overlap/preservation: fill live allocations with distinct byte patterns
   and verify them after writes, allocations, and frees. test_correctness also
   runs 3000 deterministic operations on 32 slots with sizes 1..64, checking
   every live object's contents before every operation.
3. Exact-fit allocation: allocate/write/free all 4088 payload bytes. Then use
   an exact-size interior hole while all remaining heap space is allocated.
   test_correctness.
4. Small remainder: allocate 4080 bytes, free, then request 4088; detects lost
   tail space when an eight-byte remainder cannot form a chunk. test_correctness.
5. Reuse/coalescing: free adjacent 64-byte chunks while a guard object exhausts
   the rest of the heap, request their combined 136-byte payload, and verify
   guard contents. Also merge with both free neighbors to form 208 payload
   bytes. Finally free all and request 4088 bytes. test_correctness.
6. Initialization: first operation is free(NULL); other test processes begin
   with allocation or an invalid free, with no explicit initialization call.
7. Invalid frees: independent processes test an outside pointer, an interior
   pointer, immediate double-free, and double-free after coalescing. The runner
   verifies status 2 and the stderr message with source filename and line.
8. Exhaustion/boundaries: request 4088 then another byte; also request 4089,
   SIZE_MAX, and zero. Check NULL, status zero, diagnostic format, request size,
   and absence of leak messages. Check successful full allocation after freeing.
9. Leak detector: intentionally leave 1-byte and 20-byte objects live while
   freeing another; expect exactly 32 payload bytes in two objects. A separate
   no-leak case must produce no stderr output.
10. Stress: run memgrind, expect status zero, formatted timing output, and empty
    stderr (including no leak report).

TEST PROGRAMS
test_correctness: no arguments; assert-based positive tests, prints success.
test_errors: exactly one case argument:
  outside, interior, double, merged-double: expected exit status 2.
  oom, oversize, overflow, zero: expected allocation diagnostic, status 0.
  leak: expected 'mymalloc: 32 bytes leaked in 2 objects.', status 0.
  no-leak, null: expected no diagnostic, status 0.
The interior-pointer test terminates while its object remains allocated, so
an additional leak report on exit is expected. Unknown/missing cases exit 1.
run_tests.sh runs each case separately and checks status and output.

